WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Welcome to UFI Career Studio. Build behavioral stories

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.040
that land. Use a simple story structure to

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.561
show what you owned, how you decided, and

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:14.081
what changed because of it. We will work

00:00:14.081 --> 00:00:17.601
through five practical steps, with examples you can

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:19.801
adapt to your own experience.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.242
Set the scene with only the context needed

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:27.817
to understand the challenge. Name the setting, the

00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:31.392
problem, and why it mattered. For example, a

00:00:31.392 --> 00:00:34.967
team had a deadline approaching and two departments

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:38.542
were working from different requirements. Avoid spending most

00:00:38.542 --> 00:00:42.117
of the answer explaining the organization. The interviewer

00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:45.692
needs enough context to understand your decisions, not

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:47.926
every detail of the project.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.600
Explain what you personally owned. Were you responsible

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.449
for a decision, a deliverable, or keeping people

00:00:56.449 --> 00:01:00.299
aligned? Separate your responsibility from the team goal.

00:01:00.299 --> 00:01:04.148
If the situation was difficult, name the constraint,

00:01:04.148 --> 00:01:07.998
such as limited time or incomplete information. This

00:01:07.998 --> 00:01:11.848
makes it possible for the interviewer to evaluate

00:01:11.848 --> 00:01:15.697
your contribution fairly instead of guessing what you

00:01:15.697 --> 00:01:16.660
actually did.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.771
Spend the largest part of the story on

00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:24.042
your actions and reasoning. Describe the options you

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:27.313
considered, the choice you made, and how you

00:01:27.313 --> 00:01:30.585
communicated it. Use I for your own work

00:01:30.585 --> 00:01:33.856
and we for shared work. If you disagreed

00:01:33.856 --> 00:01:37.127
with someone, explain how you handled the issue

00:01:37.127 --> 00:01:40.398
respectfully. A thoughtful decision process can be valuable

00:01:40.398 --> 00:01:43.260
even when the outcome was not perfect.

00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:47.472
Close with what changed and what you learned.

00:01:47.472 --> 00:01:50.860
Use a real measure when available, but a

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.248
clear qualitative result is also useful. If the

00:01:54.248 --> 00:01:57.636
project failed, explain what you would do differently

00:01:57.636 --> 00:02:01.025
and how you applied that lesson later. Avoid

00:02:01.025 --> 00:02:04.413
turning every story into an unrealistic success. Honest

00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:07.801
reflection gives the interviewer evidence of how you

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.919
learn and respond to setbacks.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.281
Build a small story bank covering collaboration, conflict,

00:02:14.281 --> 00:02:17.812
ambiguity, a mistake, and an achievement. Write a

00:02:17.812 --> 00:02:21.343
few notes for situation, task, action, and result

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:24.874
for each one. Then practice adapting a story

00:02:24.874 --> 00:02:28.405
to different questions without changing the facts. Your

00:02:28.405 --> 00:02:31.935
next step is to choose one example and

00:02:31.935 --> 00:02:35.466
check that the action section explains your decisions,

00:02:35.466 --> 00:02:38.115
not just a sequence of events.
